WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.340
- The Stream for those of y'all

00:00:01.340 --> 00:00:03.220
who have not been here before.

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:05.860
This is a way for us all to stay connected

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:09.007
as you have now school
closures around the world.

00:00:09.007 --> 00:00:11.530
Khan Academy, we're a not
for profit with the mission

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:13.360
of providing a free world class education

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:14.510
for anyone, anywhere.

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:16.270
And the way we've been
doing that for years

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:19.940
is by making resources
from as early as pre-K,

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:22.130
Khan Academy Kids that
goes through Kindergarten

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:24.793
and first grade, and then
resources in Math, English,

00:00:24.793 --> 00:00:27.720
Language Arts, Science, Humanities,

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:29.330
all the way through elementary, middle,

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:32.630
high school, and even the
foundations of college.

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:34.822
That's always been there,
but when the school closures

00:00:34.822 --> 00:00:36.600
started happening a few weeks ago,

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:39.010
we realized it's our duty to do more.

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:41.140
So we've been doing parent webinars,

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:43.470
and teacher webinars
with daily live stream

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:44.530
to stay connected.

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:47.029
And we've been publishing
things like schedules

00:00:47.029 --> 00:00:49.050
for students of different age groups.

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:51.270
And over the course of the
next few hours, actually,

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:53.456
we're gonna be publishing some
learning plans for students

00:00:53.456 --> 00:00:55.470
at different grade levels,

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:57.570
for how you can learn through
the end of the school year,

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:00.980
and keep learning, ideally,
through the summer.

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:04.500
I do wanna remind folks,
we are a not for profit.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.040
And we're funding by
philanthropic donations

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:08.620
from folks like yourself.

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:10.190
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:12.610
please think about
donating to Khan Academy.

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:14.280
I do wanna give a quick shout out

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:15.640
to several corporations

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:17.580
who've stepped up in the last few weeks

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:19.117
when they saw that our.

00:01:25.409 --> 00:01:30.409
Folks like Bank of America,
AT&amp;T, Google.org, and Novartis.

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:32.557
And I wanna thank our
many other supporters.

00:01:32.557 --> 00:01:35.020
But even with that, we
were running at a deficit

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:37.830
before the crisis hit.

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:41.810
And the costs have gone even higher.

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:45.380
But with that, I'd like to
introduce our guest for today.

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:49.850
David Coleman from the College Board,

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:52.300
CEO of the College Board,
another not for profit

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:54.910
that we have been close
partners with for many years

00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:56.740
around official SAT practice.

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:58.960
And David, maybe a good place to start,

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:00.460
even before our live stream started,

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:02.480
and I encourage everyone to ask questions

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:03.720
on the message boards.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:04.883
We have team members looking at it.

00:02:04.883 --> 00:02:08.580
But we had a lot of
questions about AP exams,

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:10.480
and how that's all going to work out.

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:12.310
So I'd love to hear how you
all are thinking about it,

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:14.650
and how it's going to
play out for students.

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:15.620
Thanks, Sal.

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:17.260
And I hope you and your family is well,

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:20.390
and your colleagues are well
through this demanding time.

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:21.770
I know people are working at home

00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:23.880
and facing the demands of,
sometimes, their children

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:25.140
and other forces.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:27.223
And not to be somber,
but I know this can be,

00:02:27.223 --> 00:02:29.940
has already been for some
and will be for others,

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:31.570
a time of loss.

00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:34.681
And I just wanna offer
our heartfelt condolences

00:02:34.681 --> 00:02:38.490
and support for people who
are struggling through this.

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:41.530
And I know it sounds
weird to begin this way,

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:44.190
but there are definitely things
more important in this life

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:45.960
than AP and SAT.

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:48.940
And those include the safety
and sanctity of everyone.

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:51.800
And I know you and I
are also very concerned

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:53.367
that this crisis will hit the low income

00:02:53.367 --> 00:02:55.670
and under-represented communities hardest

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:58.040
in this country as things
like this always do.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:00.628
And that's why Khan
Academy is such a beacon,

00:03:00.628 --> 00:03:03.872
available on any device to any person.

00:03:03.872 --> 00:03:06.100
What an equalizer.

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:08.104
And I'll talk a little bit
of why the digital divide

00:03:08.104 --> 00:03:12.047
can still make that hard,
but I don't know of a force

00:03:12.047 --> 00:03:16.780
that is as astonishing as Khan Academy is

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:20.360
in offering such a high
quality to everyone.

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:22.240
When we partnered with Khan Academy,

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:24.260
it was not to provide free test prep, Sal,

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:25.130
as you said.

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:26.930
It was to provide the best test prep

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:28.430
that happened to be free.

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:30.346
And Khan Academy has delivered on that.

00:03:30.346 --> 00:03:33.873
The quality of your team's
work in everything it touches

00:03:33.873 --> 00:03:36.980
continues to amaze me and inspire me

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:38.580
and us at the College Board.

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:42.277
And my most important
message today, maybe,

00:03:42.277 --> 00:03:44.708
never has the importance of Khan Academy

00:03:44.708 --> 00:03:47.240
been more evident to the world.

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:50.180
And I just hope people listening to this

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:51.960
really understand that donating to one

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:54.367
of the great public utilities of our time,

00:03:54.367 --> 00:03:56.509
on of the great public goods of our time,

00:03:56.509 --> 00:03:59.598
which is Khan Academy,
is urgent and important.

00:03:59.598 --> 00:04:04.598
It's now evident that it is
essential to everyone alive.

00:04:04.690 --> 00:04:07.040
And let's help make it
so by investing in it.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:10.180
Know that we invest in
you due to your great work

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:12.723
in the SAT with us,

00:04:15.059 --> 00:04:16.470
and we work with a lot of players

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:19.030
in this space and we regard
none higher than Khan Academy

00:04:19.030 --> 00:04:21.000
in the quality of its work and its ethos.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:23.120
So sorry for that long preamble,

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:25.509
but I think that's really important.

00:04:25.509 --> 00:04:28.520
- No, I appreciate that, David.

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:30.740
And how are you doing?

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:33.070
I mean, it's my bad
for, you're in New York,

00:04:33.070 --> 00:04:33.903
I know your partner and--

00:04:33.903 --> 00:04:35.519
- I'm really well.

00:04:35.519 --> 00:04:37.620
My husband is an ICU nurse,

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:39.080
so I'm super proud of him.

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:40.740
He's on the front lines every day

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:43.020
and I'm trying to do my
best to keep them safe

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:44.140
and help them along.

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:46.240
And we have young twins that are five,

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:48.440
that are irreverent as ever.

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:49.960
And they're thriving.

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:53.540
I cannot complain of my
individual circumstances.

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:55.900
And I get to talk about,
as many people have,

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:58.370
my concerns about my
parents and various places

00:04:58.370 --> 00:04:59.630
and all of that.

00:04:59.630 --> 00:05:01.670
But all together, Sal, we're well.

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:03.559
And we at the College Board are energized

00:05:03.559 --> 00:05:05.700
by what we're attempting to do.

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:08.560
We've chosen to come at this crisis

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:11.040
with a spirit of
innovation and flexibility.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:14.320
And so let me outline
what we're doing about AP,

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:15.900
because I know people
have a lot of questions.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:17.747
I'm gonna apologize, I'll
read a couple of things here,

00:05:17.747 --> 00:05:20.530
because I wanna get the
facts exactly right,

00:05:20.530 --> 00:05:21.982
and then turn to your questions.

00:05:21.982 --> 00:05:25.500
I hope I've made clear
that the health and safety

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:29.285
of educators, families, is
the most important thing

00:05:29.285 --> 00:05:30.447
driving the College Board.

00:05:30.447 --> 00:05:32.670
And of course students at this time.

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:34.640
That is our top priority.

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:36.590
So as students and families

00:05:36.590 --> 00:05:38.750
and teachers navigate these challenges,

00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:40.910
we've done two pivotal things.

00:05:40.910 --> 00:05:44.874
First we have launched AP
Live to provide every student,

00:05:44.874 --> 00:05:48.918
in a regular schedule each
day, free online review

00:05:48.918 --> 00:05:52.190
and coursework around every AP course.

00:05:52.190 --> 00:05:55.630
And as of now there have
been 4.7 million views

00:05:55.630 --> 00:05:57.150
of that content.

00:05:57.150 --> 00:06:00.860
And number two, we're providing
all students the chance

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:03.460
to test safely at home this spring.

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:05.755
And I'll outline that
assessment opportunity.

00:06:05.755 --> 00:06:08.350
And to be clear, before
we made that decision,

00:06:08.350 --> 00:06:09.937
which was a hard decision
because it forces

00:06:09.937 --> 00:06:12.480
an enormous amount of work and innovation

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:15.860
on the College Board's part
to design an at-home exam.

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:20.860
But we found when we
surveyed 18,000 students,

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:24.461
that over 91% of them said
they wanted the opportunity

00:06:24.461 --> 00:06:28.000
to take an exam and claim
the credit we've earned.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:31.750
Let's remember, the crisis hit after 75%

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:33.570
of the school year was
done, so these are students

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:36.340
who've been working all
year in their AP classrooms.

00:06:36.340 --> 00:06:39.378
So we felt that we could make
only one principle decision,

00:06:39.378 --> 00:06:42.870
that we would provide
a brief, at-home exam

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:45.150
to allow students to show
what they've learned.

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:49.378
And that exam could only cover information

00:06:49.378 --> 00:06:52.950
in that first 75% of the course.

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:54.720
Units that are typically covered,

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:56.280
so we will not include the material

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:59.100
that is typically covered
in March and April

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:01.750
when schools were out of session.

00:07:01.750 --> 00:07:05.040
If you wanna learn more
about either AP Live

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:10.040
or the exams, you can
always go to CB.org/AP.

00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:15.040
Let me tell you a little
bit more about the solution.

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:18.670
It will be a 45 minute online exam at home

00:07:18.670 --> 00:07:21.250
for this year and this year alone.

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:23.350
Educator-led development communities,

00:07:23.350 --> 00:07:26.010
which are a blend of
higher education professors

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:28.720
and high school AP teachers,

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:31.080
are currently selecting the exam questions

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:32.830
that will be administered.

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:36.590
Exams will be given May 11th through 15th,

00:07:36.590 --> 00:07:38.820
and May 18th to 22nd.

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:40.990
And make up test dates will be available

00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:44.900
for each subject from June 1st to 5th,

00:07:44.900 --> 00:07:47.104
but I strongly encourage all students

00:07:47.104 --> 00:07:50.579
to take the exam in that
original testing window

00:07:50.579 --> 00:07:53.270
when we're more assured
of our full capacity

00:07:53.270 --> 00:07:54.543
to reach every student.

00:07:55.540 --> 00:07:58.430
And as I mentioned before, we recognize

00:07:58.430 --> 00:08:00.000
that the digital divide could prevent

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:02.886
some low income and rural
students from participating.

00:08:02.886 --> 00:08:05.517
Working with partners,
we're heavily investing

00:08:05.517 --> 00:08:08.450
so that these students have
the tools and connectivity

00:08:08.450 --> 00:08:12.530
and technical support they need
to review AP content online

00:08:12.530 --> 00:08:13.888
and take the exam.

00:08:13.888 --> 00:08:16.760
If you are a student who needs help

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:19.420
or you're a teacher whose
students need mobile tools

00:08:19.420 --> 00:08:22.660
or connectivity, you can
reach out to us directly

00:08:22.660 --> 00:08:25.650
to let us know by April 24th.

00:08:25.650 --> 00:08:29.820
Please complete the form at CB.org/tech,

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:31.610
CB.org/tech.

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:33.920
And I'm really proud
that 50 of my colleagues

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:36.500
have given of their time
to reach out directly

00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:38.720
to these families and
students to make sure

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:40.880
that we complete the last mile.

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:43.070
And understand the pressures
on homeless students

00:08:43.070 --> 00:08:44.930
and other students in our society

00:08:44.930 --> 00:08:46.879
that may be without devices or bandwidth,

00:08:46.879 --> 00:08:49.637
or in homes where bandwidth is scarce

00:08:49.637 --> 00:08:51.920
and devices are shared.

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:54.450
We'll continue to support
students with free resources

00:08:54.450 --> 00:08:57.170
through exam day, and
while we encourage students

00:08:57.170 --> 00:08:59.850
to wait till closer to
the test date to decide,

00:08:59.850 --> 00:09:02.620
any student now registered
for exam can choose to cancel

00:09:02.620 --> 00:09:04.895
at no charge.

00:09:04.895 --> 00:09:08.358
And I have good news, we're
confident that the vast majority

00:09:08.358 --> 00:09:11.720
of higher education institutions
will honor the credit

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:12.620
from these courses.

00:09:12.620 --> 00:09:14.460
We've gotten an overwhelming response.

00:09:14.460 --> 00:09:16.408
The University of California
just published a statement,

00:09:16.408 --> 00:09:19.189
I believe it's now up to 22
states that have endorsed

00:09:19.189 --> 00:09:21.770
this solution, and several
private institutions

00:09:21.770 --> 00:09:23.160
and more are coming in every day.

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:24.796
So there's enormous consensus in higher ed

00:09:24.796 --> 00:09:28.350
to honor the work done on these exams,

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:30.360
and give credit and placement accordingly.

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:33.090
So for seniors it will
offer credit and placement

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:35.070
for their futures if they succeed,

00:09:35.070 --> 00:09:37.050
and for juniors it will offer all of that

00:09:37.050 --> 00:09:39.330
and also distinguish themselves
on the way to college.

00:09:39.330 --> 00:09:41.950
And at a time when family's
incomes are compromised

00:09:41.950 --> 00:09:43.320
across this country,

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:45.240
we felt it was our obligation, Sal,

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:48.280
to press on innovation in
order to make sure kids

00:09:48.280 --> 00:09:50.320
didn't lose their chance
to earn the credit

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:51.960
that they had done.

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:53.474
I could say a little bit
more later if you'd like

00:09:53.474 --> 00:09:56.060
about the AP Live sessions,

00:09:56.060 --> 00:09:57.860
and I've told you you
can go to the website

00:09:57.860 --> 00:09:58.740
and look at it.

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:00.480
I just wanna be clear though,

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:02.560
that nothing that happens in AP Live,

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:04.260
as cool as I think it is,

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:05.960
will be required on the exam.

00:10:05.960 --> 00:10:07.790
We know some kids will not have access

00:10:07.790 --> 00:10:09.240
to future online learning,

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:12.130
so the exams only cover
what you've already done

00:10:12.130 --> 00:10:14.470
in the first 75% of the course.

00:10:14.470 --> 00:10:17.640
We'll be adding optional
student practice online

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:19.681
for students to practice for free.

00:10:19.681 --> 00:10:23.870
And again, let me say a little
bit more now about the exams.

00:10:23.870 --> 00:10:25.760
They're for this year only.

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:28.840
We'll go back to our traditional AP exams.

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:30.140
These streamlined exams

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:32.390
will not require any textbook shipments,

00:10:32.390 --> 00:10:34.783
external proctors or
any additional workload.

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:39.235
Again, the exam content will
only cover topics and skills

00:10:39.235 --> 00:10:41.930
most AP teachers and
students have already covered

00:10:41.930 --> 00:10:43.860
in class by March.

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:46.859
And students will be able to
take these streamlined exams

00:10:46.859 --> 00:10:49.140
on any device they have access to.

00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:51.279
A computer, a tablet, or smartphone,

00:10:51.279 --> 00:10:53.980
they can even take a
picture on a smartphone

00:10:53.980 --> 00:10:57.290
of their hand written
work and upload that.

00:10:57.290 --> 00:11:00.923
Most exams will be given
from May 11th to the 22nd.

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:06.810
And as I said, the make up
dates will be June 1st to 5th.

00:11:06.810 --> 00:11:08.844
In the unlikely event that schools reopen,

00:11:08.844 --> 00:11:11.273
students can take these exams there.

00:11:12.550 --> 00:11:15.001
Let me say a little bit
more about the exam format.

00:11:15.001 --> 00:11:18.510
Most exams will have one or
two free response questions,

00:11:18.510 --> 00:11:21.010
and each question will
be timed separately.

00:11:21.010 --> 00:11:23.320
So students will need to write
and submit their responses

00:11:23.320 --> 00:11:25.780
within the allotted
time for each question.

00:11:25.780 --> 00:11:28.258
For most subjects, the exam
will be 45 minutes long,

00:11:28.258 --> 00:11:31.510
plus an additional five
minutes for uploading.

00:11:31.510 --> 00:11:34.230
Students will need to access
the online testing system

00:11:34.230 --> 00:11:36.608
30 minutes early to get set up.

00:11:36.608 --> 00:11:40.504
Certain courses, such
as AP Art and Design 2D,

00:11:40.504 --> 00:11:43.593
Art and Design 3D, Computer
Science Principals,

00:11:43.593 --> 00:11:46.100
Drawing, Research, and Seminar

00:11:46.100 --> 00:11:48.130
will use portfolios submission

00:11:48.130 --> 00:11:52.100
and it will not have a
separate online exam.

00:11:52.100 --> 00:11:54.520
All deadlines for these
submissions have been extended

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:59.340
to May 26th at 11:59 pm
for those who cut it close,

00:11:59.340 --> 00:12:00.670
Eastern time.

00:12:00.670 --> 00:12:01.820
Teachers and students

00:12:01.820 --> 00:12:05.580
will receive special
course-specific communications

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:07.440
on these projects.

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:09.410
And interestingly and excitingly,

00:12:09.410 --> 00:12:10.920
students taking World Language

00:12:10.920 --> 00:12:14.770
and Culture exams will complete
solely two spoken tasks,

00:12:14.770 --> 00:12:17.490
consistent with free response
questions three and four

00:12:17.490 --> 00:12:18.970
in the current AP exam.

00:12:18.970 --> 00:12:22.150
They'll be given an oral
exam by a qualified adult.

00:12:22.150 --> 00:12:24.860
And so we're really going
direct from readers to students

00:12:24.860 --> 00:12:27.550
to hear their command of the language.

00:12:27.550 --> 00:12:30.090
As you can see, what we've
tried to do is we've tried

00:12:30.090 --> 00:12:33.150
to make the exams much simpler, briefer.

00:12:33.150 --> 00:12:36.070
I want students to feel
confident that they've got this.

00:12:36.070 --> 00:12:38.300
They've done the work the first
three quarters of the exam,

00:12:38.300 --> 00:12:39.630
we're not trying to trick you,

00:12:39.630 --> 00:12:41.200
we're not trying to surprise you,

00:12:41.200 --> 00:12:43.140
we're actually trying
to just give you a venue

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:44.640
to show you what you know.

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:46.946
I've never been more confident
the majority of students

00:12:46.946 --> 00:12:49.770
should be able to flourish on these exams.

00:12:49.770 --> 00:12:52.240
And if you want, Sal,
I'll end with a few words

00:12:52.240 --> 00:12:54.527
on security, because I must.
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:12:54.527 --> 00:12:55.360
- Because of course people wonder,

00:12:55.360 --> 00:12:57.887
how can we give these
exams securely at home?

00:12:57.887 --> 00:12:59.813
Like many college level exams,

00:12:59.813 --> 00:13:02.420
they'll be open book and open note.

00:13:02.420 --> 00:13:03.900
The exam format and questions

00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:04.970
are being designed specifically

00:13:04.970 --> 00:13:06.730
for an at-home administration,

00:13:06.730 --> 00:13:08.540
so points won't be given to you

00:13:08.540 --> 00:13:09.480
for things you could find out

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:11.740
from Google or elsewhere online.

00:13:11.740 --> 00:13:14.860
However, now I must be more than clear,

00:13:14.860 --> 00:13:17.290
students may not consult
with any other individuals

00:13:17.290 --> 00:13:18.920
during the testing period.

00:13:18.920 --> 00:13:21.210
And we'll take the necessary
steps to protect the integrity

00:13:21.210 --> 00:13:23.281
of the exam administration.

00:13:23.281 --> 00:13:24.830
And, you know, I say this,

00:13:24.830 --> 00:13:27.100
I'm confident that the vast
majority of AP students

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:28.842
will follow the rules
for taking the exams.

00:13:28.842 --> 00:13:31.490
And there's something kind
of crazy about cheating

00:13:31.490 --> 00:13:33.250
on the AP exam, because
who wants to wind up

00:13:33.250 --> 00:13:35.810
in Advanced Calculus
that's not ready for it?

00:13:35.810 --> 00:13:37.968
But we will nonetheless
put in strict measures

00:13:37.968 --> 00:13:41.446
to avoid cheating, including
plagiarism software,

00:13:41.446 --> 00:13:43.261
and several other efforts.

00:13:43.261 --> 00:13:45.093
I don't wanna go into them too much depth,

00:13:45.093 --> 00:13:46.860
because the more I talk about them,

00:13:46.860 --> 00:13:48.736
they're not as strong a security measures.

00:13:48.736 --> 00:13:50.370
But they are strong.

00:13:50.370 --> 00:13:52.820
And they surround the
student in various ways.

00:13:52.820 --> 00:13:54.990
And they are taking
serious the possibility

00:13:54.990 --> 00:13:57.210
of students having someone
else impersonate themselves

00:13:57.210 --> 00:13:58.410
and show their work,

00:13:58.410 --> 00:14:01.460
or going to the internet to
share with other students

00:14:01.460 --> 00:14:03.180
or conference calls with other students.

00:14:03.180 --> 00:14:04.650
We've thought of all the scenarios

00:14:04.650 --> 00:14:06.320
and put tactics in place.

00:14:06.320 --> 00:14:09.323
But I need to warn students
in this special circumstance,

00:14:09.323 --> 00:14:11.780
the College Board will
be tougher on cheating.

00:14:11.780 --> 00:14:14.910
Students who are found cheating
in this kind of situation,

00:14:14.910 --> 00:14:18.050
we will not only, as usual,
merely cancel your score,

00:14:18.050 --> 00:14:20.280
we're gonna let every university know

00:14:20.280 --> 00:14:22.240
that you've sent an SAT score,

00:14:22.240 --> 00:14:23.590
if we have conclusive evidence,

00:14:23.590 --> 00:14:26.500
that you've sent an SAT
score or other AP scores too,

00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:28.070
that you were found cheating
in this environment.

00:14:28.070 --> 00:14:29.860
So it would be a foolish move

00:14:29.860 --> 00:14:33.200
that would limit one's further
to cheat in this environment.

00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:37.060
But that said, putting
that dark specter aside,

00:14:37.060 --> 00:14:39.040
I think this is an
extraordinary opportunity

00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:39.873
for AP students.

00:14:39.873 --> 00:14:41.780
There's free live lessons every day,

00:14:41.780 --> 00:14:43.691
brief exams that will reinforce,

00:14:43.691 --> 00:14:45.622
that will reflect your mastery.

00:14:45.622 --> 00:14:48.390
And I really look forward
to see what kids can do,

00:14:48.390 --> 00:14:49.340
and student can do.

00:14:50.275 --> 00:14:51.108
That's it, Sal,

00:14:51.108 --> 00:14:51.941
thanks for your time.
- No, that' super helpful.

00:14:51.941 --> 00:14:54.320
And I think it already
addresses a lot of the questions

00:14:54.320 --> 00:14:55.430
that we've been getting.

00:14:55.430 --> 00:14:56.470
A few things you've mentioned, though,

00:14:56.470 --> 00:14:59.330
I think are interesting
and address some questions.

00:14:59.330 --> 00:15:01.129
I think we can highlight, from Instagram,

00:15:01.129 --> 00:15:05.850
@earthly1008 asked,
I've only a mobile phone

00:15:05.850 --> 00:15:08.620
and I took AP Calc BC, so
will it be possible for me

00:15:08.620 --> 00:15:09.453
to take the exam?

00:15:09.453 --> 00:15:11.270
It sounds like it will?

00:15:11.270 --> 00:15:12.167
- It will, it will.

00:15:12.167 --> 00:15:14.100
The only disadvantage of a mobile phone

00:15:14.100 --> 00:15:16.140
is typically in the humanities,

00:15:16.140 --> 00:15:18.220
when some of the prompts
may be a little longer.

00:15:18.220 --> 00:15:20.250
It'll be a little annoying
to scroll through them

00:15:20.250 --> 00:15:21.150
on your phone.

00:15:21.150 --> 00:15:22.580
But that doesn't mean that you can either

00:15:22.580 --> 00:15:25.360
then type back your answers
on the mobile phone,

00:15:25.360 --> 00:15:27.700
or in the BC Calculus situation,

00:15:27.700 --> 00:15:29.010
that person could write their work,

00:15:29.010 --> 00:15:31.440
take a picture of it and
upload it in the phone.

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:33.059
But then they can get all
the prompts from the phone,

00:15:33.059 --> 00:15:34.567
they can get the questions
from the phone, yep.

00:15:34.567 --> 00:15:36.530
- And what about students
who don't even have that,

00:15:36.530 --> 00:15:39.175
or they may be afraid that
their phone isn't good enough?

00:15:39.175 --> 00:15:41.690
You mentioned CB.org/tech?

00:15:41.690 --> 00:15:42.710
How would that work?

00:15:42.710 --> 00:15:43.543
- That's exactly right.

00:15:43.543 --> 00:15:44.960
The way it's working,
Sal, it's really exciting.

00:15:44.960 --> 00:15:47.950
When we made the announcement,
we gave that address

00:15:47.950 --> 00:15:48.790
to every student.

00:15:48.790 --> 00:15:51.300
And 2000 thus have
gotten in touch with us,

00:15:51.300 --> 00:15:52.320
and we're getting back to them.

00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:54.380
We have thousands of Chromebooks

00:15:54.380 --> 00:15:56.590
that we're trying to make
sure those Chromebooks get

00:15:56.590 --> 00:15:58.871
to just those students who need them most,

00:15:58.871 --> 00:16:01.880
who really can't borrow a
device or need them on test day.

00:16:01.880 --> 00:16:03.548
So we're trying to really ask people

00:16:03.548 --> 00:16:05.610
to be restrained in what they ask for.

00:16:05.610 --> 00:16:07.980
We're gonna loan them out
and ask for them back.

00:16:07.980 --> 00:16:10.440
We are working with other
state and district partners

00:16:10.440 --> 00:16:13.130
to ensure that their device strategies

00:16:13.130 --> 00:16:15.200
are coordinated and they're
getting to these AP students

00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:16.033
who need them.

00:16:16.033 --> 00:16:18.530
A lot of this, Sal, as you
know is case management.

00:16:18.530 --> 00:16:19.800
And we're investing today

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:22.010
in providing wide-reaching tech support.

00:16:22.010 --> 00:16:23.490
Because sometimes it's
actually tech support

00:16:23.490 --> 00:16:24.400
that gets in the way.

00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:25.760
But if you have a concern,

00:16:25.760 --> 00:16:28.961
please go to CB.org/tech.

00:16:28.961 --> 00:16:29.980
- That's super helpful.

00:16:29.980 --> 00:16:33.948
I have a question from YouTube,
Dara Rayblott is asking,

00:16:33.948 --> 00:16:36.500
will there be practice
tests, example questions

00:16:36.500 --> 00:16:39.960
in the same style as the
new at-home questions?

00:16:39.960 --> 00:16:40.860
- Yes there will.

00:16:40.860 --> 00:16:43.260
And some of them are gonna roll
out by the end of the week,

00:16:43.260 --> 00:16:46.060
and then we will be having
more practice for you.

00:16:46.060 --> 00:16:48.346
We may even have, we're just designing now

00:16:48.346 --> 00:16:50.900
ways that you can simulate
the test experience

00:16:50.900 --> 00:16:52.030
and test the systems.

00:16:52.030 --> 00:16:55.010
And a way you can further take
practice questions as you go.

00:16:55.010 --> 00:16:57.430
So the answer is yes, already
by the end of the week

00:16:57.430 --> 00:16:59.700
there'll be practice, and
there'll be more to come

00:16:59.700 --> 00:17:00.850
as we further develop it.

00:17:00.850 --> 00:17:03.570
- And there's two questions
here that are related.

00:17:03.570 --> 00:17:05.053
One is from, they're both from YouTube.

00:17:05.053 --> 00:17:08.740
IshaKonham is asking,
will the AP exam be harder

00:17:08.740 --> 00:17:10.230
to compensate for open notes?

00:17:10.230 --> 00:17:13.470
And Lucy is asking, how is
the AP curve going to work

00:17:13.470 --> 00:17:16.120
for students if the tests
are going to be open notes?

00:17:17.174 --> 00:17:19.730
- Well, the curve is not really affected,

00:17:19.730 --> 00:17:21.570
because you're still evaluating students

00:17:21.570 --> 00:17:23.720
on a one through five scale
as to whether their work

00:17:23.720 --> 00:17:25.233
represents college-ready work

00:17:25.233 --> 00:17:27.140
in a more-or-less advance level.

00:17:27.140 --> 00:17:29.340
And we're still gonna have the
distributed reading process.

00:17:29.340 --> 00:17:31.690
So readers won't meet in person,

00:17:31.690 --> 00:17:34.518
but we'll still have college
faculty and AP teachers

00:17:34.518 --> 00:17:37.470
reading the work and
making those judgments.

00:17:37.470 --> 00:17:39.317
I think students should
enter this exam situation

00:17:39.317 --> 00:17:41.310
with great confidence.

00:17:41.310 --> 00:17:43.990
I wanna be clear that if
you look at what's happening

00:17:43.990 --> 00:17:46.580
in higher ed, and what's
happening in the AP program,

00:17:46.580 --> 00:17:49.460
we're trying to get every
chance to students today.

00:17:49.460 --> 00:17:50.293
We know that students

00:17:50.293 --> 00:17:51.943
are under very stressful circumstances,

00:17:51.943 --> 00:17:54.866
and so we're trying to make
the exam as brief as possible.

00:17:54.866 --> 00:17:56.740
We don't wanna surprise anyone,

00:17:56.740 --> 00:17:59.130
we wanna make the questions,
as much as possible,

00:17:59.130 --> 00:18:01.620
look like work already done in the class

00:18:01.620 --> 00:18:04.640
so that we can look at
that value-added analysis,

00:18:04.640 --> 00:18:06.840
you can do the synthesis,
you can do the analysis,

00:18:06.840 --> 00:18:08.900
you do not just regurgitate facts.

00:18:08.900 --> 00:18:10.890
But we're not trying
to trick you in any way

00:18:10.890 --> 00:18:13.850
and it should be very
routine with the kind of work

00:18:13.850 --> 00:18:15.893
you've already been
doing in the classroom.

00:18:17.150 --> 00:18:19.170
- And there's another
question here on technology

00:18:19.170 --> 00:18:22.830
from Facebook, alpanaloyelka asks,

00:18:22.830 --> 00:18:24.930
we have internet issues from time to time,

00:18:24.930 --> 00:18:26.880
what would be a good backup
that you would suggest

00:18:26.880 --> 00:18:29.845
in case it happens in
the middle of the exam?

00:18:29.845 --> 00:18:31.940
- I think then the make up testing

00:18:31.940 --> 00:18:33.490
is gonna be essential, Sal.

00:18:33.490 --> 00:18:35.080
So what I would like people to do,

00:18:35.080 --> 00:18:37.740
is what we're not finding
so far in our testing.

00:18:37.740 --> 00:18:40.230
I know people worry about like
the earth shattering moment

00:18:40.230 --> 00:18:41.330
the internet goes down

00:18:41.330 --> 00:18:43.110
just at the very moment they're testing.

00:18:43.110 --> 00:18:44.560
But what's more likely is people

00:18:44.560 --> 00:18:46.120
haven't checked their browsers,

00:18:46.120 --> 00:18:49.570
and haven't gone through
the exam simulation process

00:18:49.570 --> 00:18:51.920
and have a tech support
issue they didn't expect.

00:18:51.920 --> 00:18:54.640
So we will ask students
to go through simulations

00:18:54.640 --> 00:18:58.000
in advance, and I'm kind
of as much as possible

00:18:58.000 --> 00:18:59.920
asking all students to really do that,

00:18:59.920 --> 00:19:01.940
and we will monitor that closely.

00:19:01.940 --> 00:19:05.392
And second of all, as I said,
please sign up 30 minutes

00:19:05.392 --> 00:19:07.023
before the exam begins.

00:19:08.319 --> 00:19:09.710
- That's useful.

00:19:09.710 --> 00:19:10.673
And there's a lot of questions,

00:19:10.673 --> 00:19:12.210
for those of you all who don't know,

00:19:12.210 --> 00:19:14.370
the College Board, which
you all are not for profit

00:19:14.370 --> 00:19:16.810
that represents essentially
a board of colleges.

00:19:16.810 --> 00:19:18.706
You represent what the
colleges are thinking

00:19:18.706 --> 00:19:20.142
on a lot of levels.

00:19:20.142 --> 00:19:22.370
And you all are also, as
we talked about earlier,

00:19:22.370 --> 00:19:24.025
the administer of the SAT.

00:19:24.025 --> 00:19:25.820
And we're close partners on that

00:19:25.820 --> 00:19:27.560
around official SAT practice.

00:19:27.560 --> 00:19:28.860
Couple of questions on that front,

00:19:28.860 --> 00:19:31.448
but related to this notion
of potential at-home testing.

00:19:31.448 --> 00:19:34.280
From YouTube, Tom Bert is asking,

00:19:34.280 --> 00:19:38.010
is the College Board preparing
for home online SAT testing

00:19:38.010 --> 00:19:40.490
in the eventuality COVID-19 prevents

00:19:40.490 --> 00:19:42.383
using testing centers in June?

00:19:44.266 --> 00:19:47.570
- I think the eventuality
in which the College Board

00:19:47.570 --> 00:19:49.710
is preparing for, and
that I've talked about

00:19:49.710 --> 00:19:51.790
in a previous communication.

00:19:51.790 --> 00:19:53.240
So our members, Sal, just to be clear,

00:19:53.240 --> 00:19:55.550
are virtually all the
nation's high schools

00:19:55.550 --> 00:19:57.060
and all the colleges.

00:19:57.060 --> 00:19:58.680
And so we work together with high schools

00:19:58.680 --> 00:20:00.290
and colleges to make that connection.

00:20:00.290 --> 00:20:02.080
Not only domestically, but globally.

00:20:02.080 --> 00:20:03.610
And the SAT's part of that.

00:20:03.610 --> 00:20:05.520
But my brief answer to the question is,

00:20:05.520 --> 00:20:08.390
I think we will need an
at-home style solution

00:20:08.390 --> 00:20:11.457
for the SAT if schools are out this fall,

00:20:11.457 --> 00:20:13.740
and we'll be giving more updates

00:20:13.740 --> 00:20:16.270
on our progress towards
that in about a week's time.

00:20:16.270 --> 00:20:18.142
But we have announced
that we are preparing

00:20:18.142 --> 00:20:20.690
in that sad eventuality,

00:20:20.690 --> 00:20:22.520
that our society really is off-kilter

00:20:22.520 --> 00:20:24.750
and kids can't get back
into a school setting

00:20:24.750 --> 00:20:27.683
by this fall, that we
will develop alternatives.

00:20:28.550 --> 00:20:29.383
- Yep.

00:20:29.383 --> 00:20:30.500
And there's one thing
that I think you said

00:20:30.500 --> 00:20:32.281
two questions ago that I
really wanna highlight.

00:20:32.281 --> 00:20:34.349
When people are asking
about the curve of AP.

00:20:34.349 --> 00:20:36.062
It's really important
for people to recognize,

00:20:36.062 --> 00:20:39.760
if everyone who takes the
AP exam knows the material

00:20:39.760 --> 00:20:41.750
at a five level, you will
give everyone a five.

00:20:41.750 --> 00:20:43.378
I not one does, you'll all get ones.

00:20:43.378 --> 00:20:46.950
- Once again showing that
you are better at this

00:20:46.950 --> 00:20:48.110
than I am sometimes, no, kidding.

00:20:48.110 --> 00:20:50.440
Kidding aside, it's a
perfect thing Sal says,

00:20:50.440 --> 00:20:52.330
there is no curve in that sense.

00:20:52.330 --> 00:20:54.520
It's not like there's only
so many fives to give out,

00:20:54.520 --> 00:20:56.520
so many fours, so many threes.

00:20:56.520 --> 00:20:59.630
We are really trying to create a situation

00:20:59.630 --> 00:21:01.870
where students who have
done the work of this class

00:21:01.870 --> 00:21:04.003
can with confidence do well on this exam.

00:21:05.381 --> 00:21:06.214
- And there's a lot of questions--

00:21:06.214 --> 00:21:07.060
Oh, go ahead.
- I just wanna be clear

00:21:07.060 --> 00:21:08.120
about something.

00:21:08.120 --> 00:21:09.170
Oh, sorry Sal.

00:21:09.170 --> 00:21:11.950
We know that like while it's
cool that we have AP Live,

00:21:11.950 --> 00:21:14.660
that a lot of students have
other things going on right now.

00:21:14.660 --> 00:21:16.728
And they might just have
the time to take the exam.

00:21:16.728 --> 00:21:19.030
And we've really designed
this exam to make sure

00:21:19.030 --> 00:21:21.010
that even if you don't
have a bunch of time

00:21:21.010 --> 00:21:23.240
to do a lot of studying
in between now and then,

00:21:23.240 --> 00:21:24.750
you still should be able to show your work

00:21:24.750 --> 00:21:25.583
and do well.

00:21:25.583 --> 00:21:27.160
So we've tried to be very attentive

00:21:27.160 --> 00:21:30.160
to the mix of circumstances our
students find themselves in.

00:21:31.750 --> 00:21:33.780
- And we've gotten a ton of questions,

00:21:33.780 --> 00:21:36.130
not just today, but in
previous live streams

00:21:36.130 --> 00:21:38.680
around, I think it's
mainly high school juniors,

00:21:38.680 --> 00:21:40.230
who are just stressed a little bit

00:21:40.230 --> 00:21:42.850
about the college application
process generally,

00:21:42.850 --> 00:21:44.910
and given all of the volatility.

00:21:44.910 --> 00:21:46.750
They might not have, I know
some of the local districts

00:21:46.750 --> 00:21:49.223
have gone pass/fail for this term.

00:21:50.380 --> 00:21:52.633
Y'all represent these colleges

00:21:52.633 --> 00:21:54.891
and all things admissions,
and things like that.

00:21:54.891 --> 00:21:56.743
What are you hearing from them?

00:21:57.600 --> 00:22:00.250
- Yeah, I'm gonna do my best to give you

00:22:00.250 --> 00:22:02.080
as straight a shot as I can, Sal.

00:22:02.080 --> 00:22:05.840
I would urge immense calm
on the part of students

00:22:05.840 --> 00:22:07.120
for a certain reason.

00:22:07.120 --> 00:22:09.270
Colleges want them more than ever.

00:22:09.270 --> 00:22:11.730
Colleges are concerned
about filling their classes,

00:22:11.730 --> 00:22:12.870
about students coming back.

00:22:12.870 --> 00:22:15.360
So don't think it hasn't
affected everyone.

00:22:15.360 --> 00:22:18.180
So colleges are hungrier
than ever for talent,

00:22:18.180 --> 00:22:20.200
are hungrier than ever to find students.

00:22:20.200 --> 00:22:22.440
They're worried about recruiting students,

00:22:22.440 --> 00:22:24.700
they're worried about students
from various communities

00:22:24.700 --> 00:22:27.590
not being adequately represented
on their college campuses.

00:22:27.590 --> 00:22:30.046
So it's never been a better time

00:22:30.046 --> 00:22:32.210
to be a student applying to college.

00:22:32.210 --> 00:22:34.040
There's such a hunger
among the nation's colleges

00:22:34.040 --> 00:22:37.000
to re-engage with you, get
back in touch with you,

00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:38.880
and develop your plans with you.

00:22:38.880 --> 00:22:40.620
Don't sweat the small stuff.

00:22:40.620 --> 00:22:44.040
A pass/fail grade, or taking
the SAT later in the year

00:22:44.040 --> 00:22:45.480
than you expected.

00:22:45.480 --> 00:22:48.122
You know, claim your opportunity
with AP, get it done.

00:22:48.122 --> 00:22:53.122
But this is a time where everyone
realizes what's important.

00:22:53.306 --> 00:22:57.180
And all I would tell you,
is if there's a blessing

00:22:57.180 --> 00:22:58.530
at a time like this,

00:22:58.530 --> 00:23:00.860
from what I know about college admissions,

00:23:00.860 --> 00:23:02.312
is high school students,

00:23:02.312 --> 00:23:05.500
many of them could do with
being a lot less busy, Sal.

00:23:05.500 --> 00:23:08.470
Sometimes applying to
colleges becomes this frenzy

00:23:08.470 --> 00:23:09.830
of doing tons of activities,

00:23:09.830 --> 00:23:12.890
and trying to distinguish
yourself every which way.

00:23:12.890 --> 00:23:14.358
And the truth of college admissions

00:23:14.358 --> 00:23:17.070
is doing very well at your school work

00:23:17.070 --> 00:23:19.400
and doing one thing
outside of it that you love

00:23:19.400 --> 00:23:21.745
and show your strength
in, is really enough.

00:23:21.745 --> 00:23:26.060
And I hope that as we realize this,

00:23:26.060 --> 00:23:29.460
this crisis makes clear, that
real love for a few things

00:23:29.460 --> 00:23:32.840
matters more than being
busy and time with family.

00:23:32.840 --> 00:23:35.970
And I hope that we come back
to a more balanced approach

00:23:35.970 --> 00:23:37.773
as we return to schooling this fall.

00:23:38.918 --> 00:23:39.751
- And that's a good segue, David.

00:23:39.751 --> 00:23:41.350
Now I want to give you a chance,

00:23:41.350 --> 00:23:44.660
and I applaud what you and
the entire College Board team

00:23:44.660 --> 00:23:47.460
is doing, I can't imagine
all of the complexities

00:23:47.460 --> 00:23:49.350
you're trying to navigate right now,

00:23:49.350 --> 00:23:50.850
in record time.

00:23:50.850 --> 00:23:53.810
But I always, for those of
you all who don't realize,

00:23:53.810 --> 00:23:56.300
David and I are actually
quite good friends,

00:23:56.300 --> 00:23:57.650
over the course of many years,

00:23:57.650 --> 00:23:59.710
we've had many late night conversations

00:23:59.710 --> 00:24:03.348
where I'm trying to
expand David's imagination

00:24:03.348 --> 00:24:05.232
in one direction or another. (laughs)

00:24:05.232 --> 00:24:07.315
And he's doing the same.

00:24:07.315 --> 00:24:09.744
- No charge for the call, for the record.

00:24:09.744 --> 00:24:11.889
- (laughs) No, maybe we'll get into it.

00:24:11.889 --> 00:24:13.900
But what I always tell people,

00:24:13.900 --> 00:24:16.440
even when we first partnered
with the College Board,

00:24:16.440 --> 00:24:17.577
and people are like,
"Oh, the College Board,

00:24:17.577 --> 00:24:19.990
"that's that big institution
that administers these tests,"

00:24:19.990 --> 00:24:20.823
and all this.

00:24:20.823 --> 00:24:22.070
I was like, you gotta meet David,

00:24:22.070 --> 00:24:24.750
he's not what you're imagining as the CEO,

00:24:24.750 --> 00:24:26.050
the president of the College Board.

00:24:26.050 --> 00:24:27.780
And frankly, everyone
at the College Board,

00:24:27.780 --> 00:24:31.570
are these really thoughtful
people who are really out there

00:24:31.570 --> 00:24:33.700
that just do right by you the students,

00:24:33.700 --> 00:24:35.220
and the teachers, and the parents.

00:24:35.220 --> 00:24:37.810
And so David, and I always admired you

00:24:37.810 --> 00:24:40.390
for your ability to
think broadly about life

00:24:40.390 --> 00:24:43.915
and where we sit and the
history and things like that.

00:24:43.915 --> 00:24:47.106
What advice do you have for
everyone going through this

00:24:47.106 --> 00:24:50.123
as people, but especially
as students right now?

00:24:55.490 --> 00:24:58.552
- Many people will say to you
that everything is changed.

00:24:58.552 --> 00:25:01.963
And so I wanna start by
what remains the same.

00:25:03.250 --> 00:25:07.010
The most powerful thing
you can do as a student

00:25:07.010 --> 00:25:09.757
is to develop authentic fascinations.

00:25:09.757 --> 00:25:12.490
And what I hope AP is for students

00:25:12.490 --> 00:25:15.890
is a chance to gain the
pleasure and challenge

00:25:15.890 --> 00:25:17.710
of getting really good at something.

00:25:17.710 --> 00:25:19.970
And I know that sounds super simple,

00:25:19.970 --> 00:25:22.650
but I want you to think for a moment

00:25:22.650 --> 00:25:25.680
about this one precious
life you've been given.

00:25:25.680 --> 00:25:28.520
And how meaningful it is to
get fascinated by something,

00:25:28.520 --> 00:25:30.250
to fall into something.

00:25:30.250 --> 00:25:32.740
And when you think about
applying to college,

00:25:32.740 --> 00:25:35.080
if you think, boy, what
does it really take?

00:25:35.080 --> 00:25:36.990
It takes getting to know
your teachers really well

00:25:36.990 --> 00:25:38.780
and finding some great teachers.

00:25:38.780 --> 00:25:41.160
Because those mentors
are everything to us.

00:25:41.160 --> 00:25:44.320
It means falling in love
with your academic work,

00:25:44.320 --> 00:25:46.420
to some degree, but particularly
certain parts of it.

00:25:46.420 --> 00:25:48.556
And then it's finding an
activity outside of school

00:25:48.556 --> 00:25:50.930
that you devote yourself to.

00:25:50.930 --> 00:25:53.650
And what's funny, Sal,
is before the crisis,

00:25:53.650 --> 00:25:55.840
we have research on success at college

00:25:55.840 --> 00:25:59.000
that says if you do those
things when you get to college,

00:25:59.000 --> 00:26:00.400
you're gonna have a great experience.

00:26:00.400 --> 00:26:03.735
If you find great teachers,
if you find a class

00:26:03.735 --> 00:26:06.770
or discipline that you love
and apply to your life,

00:26:06.770 --> 00:26:09.580
and if you love an
activity outside of class.

00:26:09.580 --> 00:26:11.500
So, I know that sounds strange,

00:26:11.500 --> 00:26:15.200
but it seems to me that
success remains as simple

00:26:15.200 --> 00:26:17.250
as kind of falling in love three times.

00:26:17.250 --> 00:26:20.240
You know, being devoted to your teachers

00:26:20.240 --> 00:26:22.545
and the relationships you have with them.

00:26:22.545 --> 00:26:25.020
To disciplines and finding academic work

00:26:25.020 --> 00:26:27.170
that really makes you awake at night

00:26:27.170 --> 00:26:28.600
and excites you.

00:26:28.600 --> 00:26:32.232
And finally, finding an
activity that engages you.

00:26:32.232 --> 00:26:35.061
And when Sal and I look at each other

00:26:35.061 --> 00:26:37.860
and care for one another, it's in part

00:26:37.860 --> 00:26:39.980
because we both love what we do.

00:26:39.980 --> 00:26:43.940
Sal is a man energized
entirely by passion.

00:26:43.940 --> 00:26:47.670
He began Khan Academy
sharing his delight in math

00:26:47.670 --> 00:26:48.930
with his nephew and nieces.

00:26:48.930 --> 00:26:51.470
It began as an act of love,

00:26:51.470 --> 00:26:54.070
and as kind of irrepressible desire

00:26:54.070 --> 00:26:57.200
to bring other people
into the beauty and power

00:26:57.200 --> 00:26:59.120
that he found in mathematics.

00:26:59.120 --> 00:27:02.320
Sal's passion for that changed the world.

00:27:02.320 --> 00:27:05.754
And that cultivation of
passion in excellence

00:27:05.754 --> 00:27:07.648
is such a winning hand.

00:27:07.648 --> 00:27:11.350
Faking it, being inauthentic,
putting a face on yourself

00:27:11.350 --> 00:27:13.790
for college applications,
getting caught up

00:27:13.790 --> 00:27:17.150
and doing too many things,
wastes the precious life

00:27:17.150 --> 00:27:18.150
we've been given.

00:27:18.150 --> 00:27:21.080
But working hard to
discover what you love,

00:27:21.080 --> 00:27:22.856
finding teacher that really move you,

00:27:22.856 --> 00:27:24.904
these are lasting things.

00:27:24.904 --> 00:27:27.880
And what you'll find is later in life,

00:27:27.880 --> 00:27:30.290
you know, Sal says he's
impressed with the responsiveness

00:27:30.290 --> 00:27:32.820
of the College Board, and
honestly I kind of am too.

00:27:32.820 --> 00:27:34.650
A lot of people have canceled exams,

00:27:34.650 --> 00:27:37.220
and we came to this with
a very innovative can-do.

00:27:37.220 --> 00:27:39.080
We're gonna make it work at home attitude.

00:27:39.080 --> 00:27:40.920
But that's in part
because we've been working

00:27:40.920 --> 00:27:42.720
with Khan Academy for years.

00:27:42.720 --> 00:27:45.730
And we've seen their
agility, and they inspire us.

00:27:45.730 --> 00:27:50.730
And so I think that we may be
more distant from one another

00:27:51.460 --> 00:27:53.020
for a period of time,

00:27:53.020 --> 00:27:55.790
but what really matters
remains strangely the same.

00:27:55.790 --> 00:27:58.800
And never has your own motivation,

00:27:58.800 --> 00:28:02.510
your own inner drive been
more important than now.

00:28:02.510 --> 00:28:04.170
Out of the presence of your teachers,

00:28:04.170 --> 00:28:07.580
out of the immediate pressures
of applying to college,

00:28:07.580 --> 00:28:10.029
can you cultivate that internal flame

00:28:10.029 --> 00:28:13.730
that really is what delivers
you most things of value

00:28:13.730 --> 00:28:14.920
in this life.

00:28:14.920 --> 00:28:18.000
So that's my pompous
preaching for the day, Sal.

00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:19.439
I'm done.

00:28:19.439 --> 00:28:21.720
- I think that's gonna
leave a lot of students,

00:28:21.720 --> 00:28:24.510
and some of us adults, inspired.

00:28:24.510 --> 00:28:26.051
And I think it's exactly right.

00:28:26.051 --> 00:28:29.770
I think the first few weeks
of this social distancing,

00:28:29.770 --> 00:28:33.480
all of us were fixated on a
thing that we were losing.

00:28:33.480 --> 00:28:36.680
But now I think, and I know some people

00:28:36.680 --> 00:28:39.080
are in very hard situations,
and everyone is going through

00:28:39.080 --> 00:28:41.560
a lot of anxiety and stress,
but we're starting to realize

00:28:41.560 --> 00:28:42.770
that there's some silver linings:

00:28:42.770 --> 00:28:46.840
more time with family, a lot
of the noise of every day life

00:28:46.840 --> 00:28:50.030
has gone away, and we can reflect more.

00:28:50.030 --> 00:28:51.860
More times with ourselves to think about

00:28:51.860 --> 00:28:54.380
what makes us tick, who are we really?

00:28:54.380 --> 00:28:56.261
And I think for high school
students, especially,

00:28:56.261 --> 00:29:00.620
the normal world is, there's
just so much going on

00:29:00.620 --> 00:29:01.453
in their life.

00:29:01.453 --> 00:29:02.900
They don't have the time to reflect.

00:29:02.900 --> 00:29:05.501
And this generation,
all of y'all out there,

00:29:05.501 --> 00:29:08.220
actually there might be a silver lining

00:29:08.220 --> 00:29:10.390
where you have an opportunity
to do what David mentioned.

00:29:10.390 --> 00:29:12.420
You have the time to reflect who you are,

00:29:12.420 --> 00:29:15.410
what really energizes you,
and be your authentic self

00:29:15.410 --> 00:29:17.736
as you apply for college.

00:29:17.736 --> 00:29:20.960
So thanks David for joining us.

00:29:20.960 --> 00:29:22.216
I hope we can have him on again.

00:29:22.216 --> 00:29:24.090
We got a lot of questions today,

00:29:24.090 --> 00:29:26.200
more so than normal so
I know there's a lot

00:29:26.200 --> 00:29:28.300
of high school students
out there thinking a lot

00:29:28.300 --> 00:29:29.260
about college admissions

00:29:29.260 --> 00:29:30.650
and APs and SATs.

00:29:30.650 --> 00:29:34.269
I always applaud David for his ability

00:29:34.269 --> 00:29:37.042
to really speak to the
heart, as you just saw.

00:29:37.042 --> 00:29:39.007
Thanks everyone for joining.

00:29:39.007 --> 00:29:41.408
As I said, this is a way for
all of us to stay connected.

00:29:41.408 --> 00:29:43.440
Not just for y'all to stay connected,

00:29:43.440 --> 00:29:45.472
but I also get a lot of personal joy

00:29:45.472 --> 00:29:47.720
that I'm also also socially distanced,

00:29:47.720 --> 00:29:49.350
to be able to connect with all of y'all.

00:29:49.350 --> 00:29:51.970
And as a reminder, we are not for profit,

00:29:51.970 --> 00:29:53.170
if you're in a position to do so,

00:29:53.170 --> 00:29:55.870
please think about
donating to Khan Academy

00:29:55.870 --> 00:29:59.240
so that we can continue to
support the world, hopefully,

00:29:59.240 --> 00:30:02.220
in this time of crisis, and beyond.

00:30:02.220 --> 00:30:03.367
Have a good day.

